    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**Memory leak incident** is a memory-management concern. Explain allocation/retention behavior, how to detect the issue, what reference keeps objects alive, and how to reduce retention without trading away correctness.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nIdentify the user-visible symptom or engineering bottleneck, define the source of truth, change one variable, and measure the result before adding more complexity.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThis version exposes the loop, condition, state transition, or calculation so you can explain the logic line by line during the interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Resource {\n  id: string;\n  active: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction activeResources(\n  resources: readonly Resource[],\n): Resource[] {\n  const result: Resource[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < resources.length; i += 1) {\n    if (resources[i].active) {\n      result.push(resources[i]);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n\nconst active = activeResources([\n  { id: \"1\", active: true },\n  { id: \"2\", active: false },\n]);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or platform helpers when they make the same behavior clearer and the underlying performance trade-off is already understood.\n\n```ts\nconst resources = [\n  { id: \"1\", active: true },\n  { id: \"2\", active: false },\n];\n\nconst active = resources.filter(\n  (resource) => resource.active,\n);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse Memory leak incident during a measured memory investigation: reproduce, capture evidence, identify retention, remove the reference, and verify that the heap returns to a stable level.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and measurement:\nExplain the dominant cost, then validate it with the appropriate tool—build analysis, browser trace, RUM, network waterfall, memory evidence, or mobile-device testing.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nTest cold and warm paths, cache misses, failures, slow networks, low-end devices, repeated interactions, large inputs, and rollback/recovery behavior where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nState the bottleneck, evidence, implementation choice, trade-off, and the guardrail that prevents the regression from returning.",
